# Example Caddy config for a single-hotel RestaurantOS pilot VPS.
#
# Caddy over nginx here specifically because it gets you automatic
# Let's Encrypt HTTPS (issuance + renewal) with zero extra tooling --
# no certbot, no cron-renewal script, no separate ACME client to keep
# patched. For a one-VPS pilot with no existing nginx investment, that
# removes an entire operational surface nginx would otherwise add back.
# Revisit if the pilot grows into a multi-node/CDN-fronted setup where
# nginx's ecosystem (or a managed load balancer) starts to matter more
# than setup simplicity.
#
# Copy to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile on the VPS, replace the two domains
# below, and `systemctl reload caddy`. See
# docs/RestaurantOS_Production_Deployment_Guide.md "HTTPS" section.

# Staff/admin app (apps/admin-web, run via
# infrastructure/systemd/restaurantos-web.service.example on 127.0.0.1:3000).
admin.your-hotel-domain.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
}

# API (services/api, run via docker-compose.prod.yml on 127.0.0.1:8000).
# Guest QR ordering hits this origin directly from customers' phones
# (apps/admin-web's (guest) route group calls it as an API, not through
# the admin app's own origin) -- if guest ordering is served from the
# same admin-web deployment, api.* only needs to be reachable by
# admin-web's server-side fetches and the guest's browser, not
# separately hardened beyond what CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS already does.
api.your-hotel-domain.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}
